## Exclusive Write Access with &mut

Use `&mut` to create a mutable reference. Only ONE write borrow can exist at a time:

```xiom
fn add_item(v: &mut Vec[Int], item: Int) {
  v.push(item);  // Can modify through &mut
}

fn main() -> Int {
  var my_vec = Vec[Int].new();
  my_vec.push(1);
  add_item(&mut my_vec, 2);  // Give exclusive write access
  io.println(my_vec.len());   // 2
  return my_vec.len() as Int;
}
```

### &mut Is Exclusive

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int].new();
  data.push(1);

  let r1 = &mut data;  // Write borrow — exclusive!
  // let r2 = &data;   // ERROR: can't read while &mut exists
  // let r2 = &mut data; // ERROR: can't have two &mut
  r1.push(2);
  return data.len() as Int;
}
```

### No Read Borrows During Write

When you have `&mut`, ALL other borrows are forbidden — even read borrows. This prevents the classic "iterator invalidation" bug where data changes while you're reading it.

### The Write Borrow Guarantees

- No other references exist while &mut is live
- The data won't change unexpectedly during a write
- The data won't be read in an inconsistent state

